● Injected adaptive memory context into system prompt

● <adaptive_rules>
  - The program must support the listed textual commands: `quit`, `show barn`, `show board`, `show
  market`, `sell all`, `sell <Vegetable>+`, `buy vegetable <Gemüsename>`, `buy land <x> <y>`,
  `harvest <x> <y> <Anzahl>`, `plant <x> <y> <Gemüsename>`, and `end turn`.
  - Initialization input constraints: number of players must be integer >= 1; start gold must be
  integer >= 0; winning amount must be integer >= 1; seed must be integer within
  [-2147483648,2147483647].
  - Output for `show barn` and `show market` must right-align numbers so the largest number is
  separated from any colon by exactly one space; only vegetables with count > 0 are shown; the
  separator line length equals the longest line.
  - Selling zero vegetables is allowed (i.e., selling 0 is permitted).
  - The initial (pre-shuffle) order of tiles must exactly follow: 2n Garden, 3n Field, 2n Large
  Field, 2n Forest, n Large Forest.
  - On program start the program must first output a pixel-art banner, then prompt (in order) for
  number of players, names, start capital, winning amount, and seed.
  - The board display must render each tile as three lines high and five characters wide, separated
  by a `|` per line; content between `|` characters must be centered and shifted right by one
  character when needed; a missing countdown is shown as `*`.
  - In `plant <x> <y> <Gemüsename>` a successful plant operation produces no output.
  - Selling and harvesting messages must distinguish singular and plural forms (examples: `You have
  sold 1 vegetable for ... gold.` vs `You have sold n vegetables for ... gold.` and `You have
  harvested 1 <Singular>.` vs `You have harvested n <Plural>.`).
  - Winner announcement must follow one of three schemas depending on number of winners: `<player
  name> has won!`, `<player name> and <player name> have won!`, or `<player name>, ..., <player
  name> and <player name> have won!`.
  - A blank line must be printed between players' turns for readability.
  - All other Checkstyle rules must be followed.
  - All variable, method, and package naming rules must be followed.
  - All whitespace rules must be followed.
  - Submission must use Java SE 17.
  - Submission must use only the permitted Java library elements: java.util.Scanner and elements
  from java.lang, java.io, java.util, java.util.regex, java.util.stream and java.util.function.
  - Submit *.java files for task A with the correct folder structure.
  - Work must be individual (single-person) for the submission.
  - Outputs must exactly match the specification including case, spaces, and newlines; no additional
   information may be printed.
  - Placeholders shown in examples (e.g., [player name]) are never part of actual program output.
  - The dollar sign at the end of a line in examples denotes a visible space and is not part of
  actual output.
  - Bei gleicher Anzahl werden Gemüseeinträge aufsteigend alphabetisch sortiert.
  - Gemüse wird aufsteigend nach Quantität sortiert.
  - After a victory the game restarts automatically; only the `quit` command actually terminates the
   program.
  - Tiles allocated to players must be taken from the shuffled list starting at index 0 (i.e.,
  remove elements beginning at index 0).
  - Program source code must be written in English (for evaluation).
  - The list of remaining tiles must be shuffled using Collections.shuffle(List<?> list, Random
  random).
  - Each player's name must match the regular expression `[A-Za-z]+`.
  - The remaining tiles must be stored in a List (e.g., an ArrayList) and must preserve the exact
  prescribed initial order.
  - Do not include personal data except your u-Kürzel; in Javadoc author tag include only your
  u-Kürzel.
  - Program code must compile without errors.
  - Appropriate visibilities must be chosen for classes, methods, and attributes.
  - Every error message must begin with the exact string "Error:" with no leading spaces.
  - Javadoc must follow the specified rules.
  - The `quit` command may be used at any time during initialization and must immediately terminate
  the program without further output.
  - Allowed seed values are the closed integer interval [-2147483648, 2147483647].
  - Program termination must not be implemented via System.exit() or similar; normal control-flow
  mechanisms must be used.
  - Input must be read from System.in unless otherwise specified.
  - Output must be written to System.out unless otherwise specified.
  - Error messages must not contain special characters such as newlines or umlauts.
  - Mandatory tests must pass before other tests are evaluated; violation of mandatory rules yields
  automatic zero points.
  - Comments must be written consistently in either English or German.
  - Violation of specially marked Checkstyle rules causes automatic zero points for the submission.
  - Do not use the default package.
  - Source code lines must not exceed 120 characters in width.
  - Choose meaningful names for all identifiers.
  - The program must be terminated by the command `quit`, and `quit` must work in every situation.
  - The program must be started without command-line arguments; if one or more arguments are
  provided an informative error message must be printed and the program must exit without further
  output.
  - After printing an error message the program must continue and wait for the next input.
  - Do not call System.exit(), Runtime.exit(), or similar methods.
